1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 {('Nf3' bulletin so we don't know the
order in which Nc3 and Nf3 were played)} c6 5. Qb3 Nbd7 6. g3 Bd6 7. Bg2 O-O (
7... dxc4 8. Qxc4 e5 9. O-O O-O 10. Bg5 $14 {Sokolsky,A-Pachman,L, Moscow 1947
(0-1, 72)}) 8. O-O Qb6 9. c5 Qxb3 10. axb3 Bc7 11. Bf4 Bxf4 12. gxf4 Nh5 13. e3
a6 ({The rest of the score is mere conjecture as the game continuation given
in the bulletin looks totally implausible:} 13... b5 14. b4 Rb8 {- why does
Black leave the a7-pawn en prise and why doesn't White take it? -} 15. h4 Kh8
16. Kh2 Ndf6 17. Ne5 g6 {- leaving the c6-pawn en prise -} 18. Bf3 Ng7 19. Rh1
Bd7 20. h5 Ngxh5 21. Bxh5 gxh5 22. Rhg1 {- the bulletin gives this as check,
suggesting that the move 15...Kh8 never happened and also that Black's next
move is unthinkable -} Ng4+ 23. Nxg4 hxg4 {and now the illegal 24 Nxd7 1-0.
Something is radically wrong.}) 14. b4 Rb8 15. h4 Re8 16. Kh2 Ndf6 17. Ne5 g6
18. Bf3 Ng7 19. Rh1 Bd7 20. h5 Ngxh5 21. Bxh5 gxh5 22. Rhg1+ Ng4+ 23. Rxg4+
hxg4 24. Nxd7 1-0

